1. She has a vivid memory of being taught how to write her address in school during Class One. (Verbatim: "I still remember being taught addresses at school like it was in class one and we were like um having to write like they gave us little envelopes to write our address on")
2. As a child, she believed only one person could live on a given road, leading to confusion when multiple classmates lived on the same street. (Verbatim: "I was like you can't live on that road I live on that road she was like i live on that road and i was like oh no only one of us can live on that road because that's my road")
3. She was shocked to learn that multiple people could live on the same road, which she found deeply surprising. (Verbatim: "teacher miss we have a problem we all we all live on the same road but we can't all live on the same road because you know you only have one road")
4. She had difficulty accepting that multiple people could share the same first name, requiring a suffix system to differentiate them. (Verbatim: "we just took the first letter of their surname and then it was like you know name and then they were just different")
5. She once believed that her parents’ names could not be used by others, showing a childlike insistence on exclusive naming. (Verbatim: "that's not mom's name mom is mom and i remember the person being like oh i'm sorry i'm sorry you're right")
6. She recalls being confused by others referring to their parents by their actual names, which she found illogical. (Verbatim: "i remember i straight up was like that's not mom's name mom is mom and i remember the person being like oh i'm sorry i'm sorry you're right")
